{% comment %}
  Appx — Product Specs Table
  Resolves the current product to its assigned template HANDLE via the shop-level
  routing map (feature 43, context/features/43-storefront-routing-resolution.md).

  Params (render scope is isolated — both passed explicitly):
  - product: the current product
  - routing: the parsed `shop.metafields["$app"].routing.value` json object, in the
             COMPACT delivery shape written by `compactRoutingForDelivery`
             (app/utils/routingProjection.ts):
               { v, handles[], def, byType, byVendor, byCollection, byProduct,
                 excluded }
             Every by*/def value is an INDEX into `handles`; `excluded` is a
             membership set `{ "<id>": 1 }`. This is a private wire contract between
             that function and this snippet — the two must move together.

  Emits: the resolved template handle STRING, or nothing. Liquid render scope is
  isolated (a snippet cannot return a value), so the block CAPTURES this output.

  Resolution order (data-model §9, feature 45 Decision B). The ACTIVE rule set is
  disjoint (feature 42), so at most one key matches — the order is efficiency, never
  precedence. An EXPLICIT single-product assignment (`byProduct`) is checked BEFORE
  the exclude gate, so an excluded product still reaches its own dedicated table
  (the "all products EXCEPT X, and X gets its own table" story); the exclude gate
  then only carves products out of the BROAD tiers:
    byProduct[<product id>]
      -> excluded? -> nothing
      -> byType[type] -> byVendor[vendor]
        -> byCollection[<collection id>] (scan) -> def
  Each tier resolves to a handle INDEX (`hidx`); `handles[hidx]` is dereferenced
  ONCE at the end. This map is now the ONLY source of an assignment. The per-product
  override metafield that used to sit ahead of it in the block was removed 2026-08-04
  (see `blocks/spec_table.liquid`), so `byProduct` is the whole of the
  explicit-single-product story.

  COMPACT-SHAPE NOTES (Option 1, data-model §9/§14):
  - Keys are the BARE numeric id, so we build `'' | append: product.id` (forces a
    string key to match the JSON object keys) — NOT the old `gid://...` token. The
    `'' | append:` idiom is what the old code already relied on for its GID keys.
  - A tier lookup returns a handle INDEX. The test is `!= blank`, never truthiness:
    index 0 is a valid handle and `0 == blank` is false in Liquid, but an absent key
    is nil (blank). Resolving to an index first and dereferencing `handles` once, at
    the end, guards against `handles[nil]` coercing nil -> 0 and returning the wrong
    table on a miss.
  - byType / byVendor keys are raw selector strings (a blank product type/vendor
    indexes a key that never exists, so it falls through). TAG routing is intentionally
    NOT read — the compact shape omits `byTag` entirely (post-MVP; always empty).

  Collections are scanned in 50-item chunks (Shopify caps `for` at 50), breaking
  on the first hit — mirroring the block's rows loop.
{% endcomment %}
{%- liquid
  assign hidx = ''

  if routing != blank
    assign pid = '' | append: product.id

    # An explicit single-product assignment wins even for an excluded product
    # (feature 45 Decision B) — it is checked BEFORE the exclude gate.
    assign hidx = routing.byProduct[pid]

    # The broad tiers are carved out by `excluded`: an excluded product matches none
    # of them (renders nothing unless byProduct claimed it above).
    if hidx == blank
      unless routing.excluded[pid] != blank
        assign hidx = routing.byType[product.type]

        if hidx == blank
          assign hidx = routing.byVendor[product.vendor]
        endif

        if hidx == blank
          assign coll_chunks = product.collections.size | plus: 49 | divided_by: 50
          for chunk in (1..coll_chunks)
            assign coll_offset = chunk | minus: 1 | times: 50
            for collection in product.collections offset: coll_offset limit: 50
              assign cid = '' | append: collection.id
              assign coll_hit = routing.byCollection[cid]
              if coll_hit != blank
                assign hidx = coll_hit
                break
              endif
            endfor
            if hidx != blank
              break
            endif
          endfor
        endif

        if hidx == blank
          assign hidx = routing.def
        endif
      endunless
    endif
  endif

  # Dereference the handle table ONCE, guarded so a miss (nil hidx) never indexes
  # handles[0] by nil-coercion.
  assign handle = ''
  if hidx != blank
    assign handle = routing.handles[hidx]
  endif

  echo handle
-%}
